    <text>
      <body>
         <div type="doc">
            <head>
               <ref target="https://dodis.ch/52961">dodis.ch/52961</ref>
               <title type="main">Telegram<note xml:id="e5970">Telegram No. 145 (incoming translated
                     from Dutch): <idno>Netherlands Ministry of Foreign Affairs BuZa 1985–1990, blok
                        Z-299, </idno>
                            <add type="edition">
                                <idno>[NA 2.05.392]</idno>
                            </add>
                            <idno>
                        inv.nr. 4601 </idno>
                            <add type="edition">
                                <idno>[2129]</idno>
                            </add>
                     .<idno/>
                        </note> from the Dutch Ambassador in East Berlin, <persName key="P57545">Egbert Jacobs</persName>,
                  to the Dutch Minister of Foreign Affairs, <persName key="P57462">Hans van den Broek</persName>
                    </title>
               <title type="sub">
                        <choice>
                            <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                            <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                        </choice> in Final Phase</title>
            </head>
            <opener>
               <add>Confidential</add>
               <dateline>East Berlin, 4 July 1990</dateline>
            </opener>
            <p>If one could still think initially that a breather would be inserted after 1 July to
               recover, it has now become clear that the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> population sees no reason to prolong
               the continued existence of this state. The successful course of the introduction of
               the D-mark has brought more confidence in the future. Rarely is anything heard any
               more about the “insertion of own values/achievements” or about “proceeding on an
               equal footing together”. Protests about “selling out to the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>” have fallen
               silent.</p>
            <p>In this mood it has become impossible to pursue non party politics and <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> parties,
               with the exception of the <choice>
                        <abbr>PDS</abbr>
                        <expan>Partei des Demokratischen Sozialismus (Party of Democratic Socialism)</expan>
                    </choice>, have become reconciled to their insignificance. Hence
               the ever-advancing dates on which they will “join” the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> parties. Of course parties
               without an equivalent in the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> naturally feel threatened as regards their continued
               existence. Meanwhile, the farmer’s party <choice>
                        <abbr>DBP</abbr>
                        <expan>Demokratische Bauernpartei Deutschlands (Democratic Farmers' Party of Germany)</expan>
                    </choice>, having emerged as a relative winner in
               the municipal elections of 6 May, has affiliated itself with the <choice>
                        <abbr>CDU</abbr>
                        <expan>Christlich Demokratische Union Deutschlands (Christian Democratic Union of Germany)</expan>
                    </choice>. The original
               “Aufbruch” party <choice>
                        <abbr>DA</abbr>
                        <expan>Demokratischer Aufbruch (Democratic Awakening)</expan>
                    </choice> of Minister <persName key="P57572">Eppelmann</persName> is expected to follow this example soon. The <choice>
                        <abbr>DSU</abbr>
                        <expan>Deutsche Soziale Union (German Social Union)</expan>
                    </choice> is
               unable to merge with the <choice>
                        <abbr>CSU</abbr>
                        <expan>Christlich-Soziale Union in Bayern (Christian Social Union in Bavaria)</expan>
                    </choice> for political reasons emanating from Bonn and Munich and
               will go under due to internal division, fueled by reproaches about the cooperation of
               its recent leadership with <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> representatives. Right-wing Germany is clearly
               gathering in the <choice>
                        <abbr>DSU</abbr>
                        <expan>Deutsche Soziale Union (German Social Union)</expan>
                    </choice>, and because both <choice>
                        <abbr>DSU</abbr>
                        <expan>Deutsche Soziale Union (German Social Union)</expan>
                    </choice> Ministers (<persName key="P57573">Ebeling</persName>/Development Cooperation and <persName key="P57574">Diestel</persName>/Internal Affairs) have left their party
               without returning their portfolio, the <choice>
                        <abbr>DSU</abbr>
                        <expan>Deutsche Soziale Union (German Social Union)</expan>
                    </choice> is no longer represented in the
               government.</p>
            <p>Today the Minister of Justice has also left his (liberal) party “because of excessive
               influence of West German <choice>
                        <abbr>FDP</abbr>
                        <expan>Freie Demokratische Partei (Free Democratic Party)</expan>
                    </choice>”. Read: because of too much criticism from the West
               German liberal side on the past of Minister <persName key="P57575">Wünsche</persName>. That Prime Minister De
                  <persName key="P54809">Maiziere</persName> keeps these three – now non-aligned – gentlemen indicates,
               in my opinion, that he has faith in Diestel and Wünsche as competent administrators
               who are to remain until December. Both in legislative adjustment and in dismantling
               the <choice>
                        <abbr>STASI</abbr>
                        <expan>Ministerium für Staatssicherheit (Ministry for State Security of the GDR)</expan>
                    </choice>, party-political fiddling is inconvenient. Nonetheless the term “government
               crisis” has already been used, but has not led to visible political excitement, a
               sign of waning interest in the ups and downs of the government that is already seen
               as an extension of Bonn.</p>
            <p>Of the 3 conditions for joint elections at the beginning of December that have been
               brought forward by the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> coalition – the “Unification Treaty”<note xml:id="e6035">Quoted in German: <orig>Einigungsvertrag</orig>.</note>, the establishment of
               “Länder”, and the termination of the 2 + 4 consultations<note xml:id="e6038">Series
                  of meetings of the four Allied Powers (<choice>
                            <abbr>USA</abbr>
                            <expan>United States of America</expan>
                        </choice>, Britain, France, <choice>
                            <abbr>USSR</abbr>
                            <expan>Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</expan>
                        </choice>) and <choice>
                            <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                            <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                        </choice> plus
                  <choice>
                            <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                            <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                        </choice>.</note> – only the latter appears to constitute a potential problem. However,
               the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> (population) has little to do with this: in this country the issue arouses
               hardly any interest and every result appears acceptable as long as it does not delay
               unification and, secondly, brings an end to foreign patronage. If the <choice>
                        <abbr>USSR</abbr>
                        <expan>Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</expan>
                    </choice> continues
               to make trouble, this will certainly work against Moscow. The special <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>-<choice>
                        <abbr>USSR</abbr>
                        <expan>Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</expan>
                    </choice>
               connection has long been chopped off mentally by the population. Which does not mean
               that “die Wirtschaft”<note xml:id="e6041">The economy, here West-German
                  entrepreneurship.</note> has lost its interest in the markets in the east. On the
               contrary, but almost exclusively as a sales market.</p>
         </div>
